WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:07.130
MARKUS KLUTE: Welcome
back to 8.701.

00:00:07.130 --> 00:00:10.750
So we continue our discussion,
our development of QED

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.910
as a discussion of the photon.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:15.850
We have already seen how
we can describe electrons

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.040
and positrons, anti-electrons.

00:00:18.040 --> 00:00:19.570
And now it's time
to actually look

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:25.590
at the quantum of
electromagnetic fields.

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:29.730
A few general remarks first,
quantum electrodynamic

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:31.530
is a quantum field theory--

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:35.160
the quantum field
theory of electrodynamic

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:38.160
processes describing how
light interacts with matter.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.220
Specifically, all
processes where

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:46.290
a photon is used as an
exchange particle involving

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:50.250
electrically-charged particles
can be described by QED.

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:54.120
The photon is in the
limit theory particle.

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:57.700
And it's a quantum of the
electromagnetic field.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:01:00.960
But the real power in
QED lies in the fact

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:04.790
that we can describe it
as a perturbation theory.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:07.560
We'll see that we can write
down Feynman diagrams,

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:12.630
calculate them, and use those
calculations to describe

00:01:12.630 --> 00:01:15.360
processes we can
observe in experiment.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.450
And since we can do this
with a very high precision,

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:21.330
we can use QED in order to
make forecasts, in order

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.420
to understand, in order to
understand inner dynamics

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.610
of processes we can measure.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:30.480
Feynman called QED
our pride and joy.

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:36.540
And it's in the really unmatched
precision of this theory

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:40.190
where the pride and joy lies.

00:01:40.190 --> 00:01:41.620
But stepping back one step.

00:01:41.620 --> 00:01:44.540
Let's start with just
local election dynamics.

00:01:44.540 --> 00:01:46.820
And let's start from
Maxwell's equations.

00:01:46.820 --> 00:01:49.940
And I'm going to use this
just to make a few points

00:01:49.940 --> 00:01:52.370
and remarks.

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:55.610
This is really not in the
direct path of our development,

00:01:55.610 --> 00:01:57.860
but it connects the
dots to something

00:01:57.860 --> 00:02:00.170
you have already
started, meaning

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:01.850
classical electrodynamics.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.190
So you all have seen
Maxwell equations.

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:07.610
They can be expressed in
integral or differential form.

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:12.660
And you can write this even more
compact than it's given here.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:14.480
So you see Gauss's law.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.880
And you see that electric
charge generate electric fields

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.630
you can see that you
can produce currents

00:02:23.630 --> 00:02:28.340
by time changing electric
fields or by spatially

00:02:28.340 --> 00:02:30.540
changing magnetic fields.

00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:33.860
Gauss's law for
magnetic fields saying

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:37.910
that there is no magnetic
charge, no magnetic monopole,

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.710
at least as far as we know.

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.460
We have not observed those.

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.630
And then there's
Faraday's law as well.

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:47.570
You can express the magnetic
field and the electric field

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:52.230
through vector potentials.

00:02:52.230 --> 00:02:53.730
And if you go one
step further, you

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:59.330
see this very nice
form using four vectors

00:02:59.330 --> 00:03:01.190
for the potential and
the vector potential

00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.830
and the charge and the current.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.930
So what we're really
doing here right now

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:08.600
is we're just rewriting
the very same equations.

00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:14.870
And so when we use this
[INAUDIBLE] operator,

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:18.270
the form of this
equation looks like this.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:19.940
You can already see
this form is very

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:25.900
similar to the Klein-Gordon
equation we just looked at.

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.970
So let's go down this
path a little bit more.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:36.920
So this here is yet another form
to write the Maxwell equations,

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:40.310
where f is our field
strength tensor.

00:03:40.310 --> 00:03:43.230
And the field strength tenor
has all the physics involved.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.490
You see that you're describing
the electromagnetic field

00:03:46.490 --> 00:03:48.050
and its components.

00:03:48.050 --> 00:03:51.290
And then the simplification
of the Maxwell equations

00:03:51.290 --> 00:03:52.805
are sitting here.

00:03:52.805 --> 00:03:54.680
So we have the Maxwell
equation in this form.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.326
We have the Maxwell
equation in this form here.

00:03:57.326 --> 00:04:00.150
So there's one
interesting thing when

00:04:00.150 --> 00:04:03.430
we use potential in
order to describe

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:09.650
electromagnetic
processes or properties,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:15.040
is that we can actually choose
the specifics of the gauge.

00:04:15.040 --> 00:04:17.250
There's a degree
of freedom which

00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:21.029
we can choose, which doesn't
have any impact on the physics,

00:04:21.029 --> 00:04:23.770
on the reality of the physics.

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:25.410
And you can see this here.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:29.010
If you do the choice
that this component is 0,

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.740
this is called
the Coulomb gauge.

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:34.320
We basically simplify
again our Maxwell equations

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:36.140
to this point here.

00:04:36.140 --> 00:04:37.310
Great.

00:04:37.310 --> 00:04:41.510
So there's a number
of things to be said.

00:04:41.510 --> 00:04:44.430
So what we are basically
doing here, if we fix a gauge,

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:51.200
so if you fix our potential, we
tie the choice of the potential

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:53.010
to the inertial frame
we're using here.

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.340
And you could say
that's not nice.

00:04:55.340 --> 00:04:57.260
That doesn't seem
[INAUDIBLE] invariant.

00:04:57.260 --> 00:04:59.000
It is actually OK to do this.

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:01.700
The issue with that
is that you tie--

00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.890
if you go from one
frame in the other,

00:05:03.890 --> 00:05:07.430
you have to actually
change the gauge

00:05:07.430 --> 00:05:10.580
as you go along with
changing the reference frame.

00:05:10.580 --> 00:05:14.730
But there's nothing bad, it's
just a little bit awkward.

00:05:14.730 --> 00:05:19.490
All right, then moving back
so we have this equation now.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:21.540
And this equation,
obviously simplifies.

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:26.330
If there's no current or no
charge around for free photons.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:28.940
So this one, and for this
you can find the solution

00:05:28.940 --> 00:05:30.110
is again the free rates.

00:05:30.110 --> 00:05:31.610
This was the goal
of this lecture,

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:32.987
finding this free weight.

00:05:32.987 --> 00:05:34.820
So you could have
probably written this down

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:36.740
before any of the
discussion, but I just

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:38.880
want to makes some connection.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:42.710
So now this in the
QED, this a mu,

00:05:42.710 --> 00:05:45.380
becomes our big
function for the photon.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:50.930
Again, we have to this is as
a result of the we gauge made.

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:53.790
So we made specific choice
in our reference frame.

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:58.130
And then we can describe the
photon with our wave function.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:03.470
That is epsilon here, epsilon
is our polarization vector.

00:06:03.470 --> 00:06:05.820
And is a normalization factor.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.960
We always have to
normalize our wave function

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:13.160
to a specific set of unit.

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:16.240
All right, that's good.

00:06:16.240 --> 00:06:17.620
We can now analyze this.

00:06:17.620 --> 00:06:22.620
We find that those conditions
are fulfilled here,

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:25.890
basically saying that
photon decays like a photon.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:30.570
The energy of the photon is
equal to the momentum times C.

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:31.690
That's great.

00:06:31.690 --> 00:06:33.630
But it's also not a
surprise because the form

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:36.450
of this equation
here is exactly that

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:40.230
of the Klein-Gordon equation
not for massless particles.

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:42.035
In the Klein-Gordon
equation we got

00:06:42.035 --> 00:06:43.410
from this very
same relationship,

00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:47.320
so I was not surprised
that this works out.

00:06:47.320 --> 00:06:51.310
All right, one more word
on the polarization vector.

00:06:51.310 --> 00:06:53.650
So the choice you just made--

00:06:53.650 --> 00:06:55.030
I'm resulting the choice here.

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:58.750
This is our Coulomb
requires that the zeros

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:02.710
component of our
polarization vector is a0.

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:06.460
And that the polarization vector
is orthogonal to the momentum

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:08.008
vector.

00:07:08.008 --> 00:07:09.800
So in principle, you
have the three vector.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:12.560
The three vector is
perpendicular to the direction

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.710
of motion.

00:07:13.710 --> 00:07:16.640
And that allows us to find
two polarization states which

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.660
are independent of each other.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.890
So different to our
electrons before.

00:07:21.890 --> 00:07:23.360
Now we don't have four states.

00:07:23.360 --> 00:07:26.900
You only have two states
with independent solutions

00:07:26.900 --> 00:07:31.640
for a given momentum.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:33.950
All right, so with this
now, we have elections.

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:35.000
We can describe those.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.620
We can describe photons.

00:07:36.620 --> 00:07:39.830
The next step now is to look
at the Feynman rules which

00:07:39.830 --> 00:07:44.050
allows us to describe the
interaction between those two.

